; account_base.css and account_page.css are the sheet's only copy, the base
; first. The binary carries the two as strings, so a page sends the sheet with
; no file beside the binary, and the base can be answered alone to a page that
; brings its own layout. A sheet that ever contained the closing delimiter
; would end its string early and the generated module would stop compiling,
; so that mistake cannot pass quietly.
;
; A page writes the sheet inside a style element, and a browser ends that
; element at the first "</style" whatever CSS it sits in, a comment included.
; The rule fails on a sheet that holds "</", so no page is cut there.

(rule
 (target account_page_css.ml)
 (deps account_base.css account_page.css)
 (action
  (progn
   (system
    "case \"$(cat account_base.css account_page.css)\" in *'</'*) echo \"lib/auth: the account sheet holds '</', which would end the style element of a page\" >&2; exit 1 ;; esac")
   (with-stdout-to
    %{target}
    (progn
     (echo "let base = {account_page_css|")
     (cat account_base.css)
     (echo "|account_page_css}\nlet page = {account_page_css|")
     (cat account_page.css)
     (echo "|account_page_css}\nlet sheet = base ^ page\n"))))))
